{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 2, "q": "A mixture of 50 L has milk and water in the ratio 4:1. If 20 L of water is added, the new ratio is:", "opts": ["4:1","4:3","5:2","3:4"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Adding pure water changes only the water quantity — the milk quantity is untouched, so find the milk amount first (it stays fixed), then just add the extra water to the old water amount before re-forming the ratio. Milk = 4/5 × 50 = 40 L (unchanged). Water = 1/5 × 50 = 10 L, plus 20 L added = 30 L. New ratio = 40:30 = 4:3. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q219"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 3, "q": "Rice at ₹20/kg is mixed with rice at ₹30/kg so that the mixture costs ₹23/kg. The mixing ratio (cheap:dear) is:", "opts": ["1:1","20:30","7:3","3:7"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The rule of alligation says: the ratio in which two ingredients must be mixed equals the ratio of the DISTANCE of the mean price from each price, but cross-matched — cheap quantity is proportional to (dear price − mean price), and dear quantity to (mean price − cheap price). cheap:dear = (₹30−₹23):(₹23−₹20) = 7:3. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q220"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 3, "q": "Rice at ₹15/kg is mixed with rice at ₹25/kg so that the mixture costs ₹18/kg. The mixing ratio (cheap:dear) is:", "opts": ["7:3","1:1","15:25","3:7"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The rule of alligation says: the ratio in which two ingredients must be mixed equals the ratio of the DISTANCE of the mean price from each price, but cross-matched — cheap quantity is proportional to (dear price − mean price), and dear quantity to (mean price − cheap price). cheap:dear = (₹25−₹18):(₹18−₹15) = 7:3. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q221"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 3, "q": "Rice at ₹40/kg is mixed with rice at ₹60/kg so that the mixture costs ₹50/kg. The mixing ratio (cheap:dear) is:", "opts": ["1:1","40:60","1:1*","2:1"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The rule of alligation says: the ratio in which two ingredients must be mixed equals the ratio of the DISTANCE of the mean price from each price, but cross-matched — cheap quantity is proportional to (dear price − mean price), and dear quantity to (mean price − cheap price). cheap:dear = (₹60−₹50):(₹50−₹40) = 10:10 = 1:1. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q222"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 2, "q": "A mixture of 100 L has milk and water in the ratio 7:3. If 20 L of water is added, the new ratio is:", "opts": ["8:4","7:3","7:5","5:7"], "ans": 2, "exp": "Adding pure water changes only the water quantity — the milk quantity is untouched, so find the milk amount first (it stays fixed), then just add the extra water to the old water amount before re-forming the ratio. Milk = 7/10 × 100 = 70 L (unchanged). Water = 3/10 × 100 = 30 L, plus 20 L added = 50 L. New ratio = 70:50 = 7:5. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q223"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 3, "q": "Rice at ₹20/kg is mixed with rice at ₹30/kg so that the mixture costs ₹27/kg. The mixing ratio (cheap:dear) is:", "opts": ["20:30","1:1","3:7","7:3"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The rule of alligation says: the ratio in which two ingredients must be mixed equals the ratio of the DISTANCE of the mean price from each price, but cross-matched — cheap quantity is proportional to (dear price − mean price), and dear quantity to (mean price − cheap price). cheap:dear = (₹30−₹27):(₹27−₹20) = 3:7. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q224"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 2, "q": "A mixture of 100 L has milk and water in the ratio 7:3. If 10 L of water is added, the new ratio is:", "opts": ["8:4","7:3","4:7","7:4"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Adding pure water changes only the water quantity — the milk quantity is untouched, so find the milk amount first (it stays fixed), then just add the extra water to the old water amount before re-forming the ratio. Milk = 7/10 × 100 = 70 L (unchanged). Water = 3/10 × 100 = 30 L, plus 10 L added = 40 L. New ratio = 70:40 = 7:4. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q225"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 2, "q": "A mixture of 160 L has milk and water in the ratio 5:3. If 20 L of water is added, the new ratio is:", "opts": ["6:4","5:4","4:5","5:3"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Adding pure water changes only the water quantity — the milk quantity is untouched, so find the milk amount first (it stays fixed), then just add the extra water to the old water amount before re-forming the ratio. Milk = 5/8 × 160 = 100 L (unchanged). Water = 3/8 × 160 = 60 L, plus 20 L added = 80 L. New ratio = 100:80 = 5:4. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q226"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 3, "q": "Rice at ₹40/kg is mixed with rice at ₹60/kg so that the mixture costs ₹48/kg. The mixing ratio (cheap:dear) is:", "opts": ["3:2","40:60","2:3","1:1"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The rule of alligation says: the ratio in which two ingredients must be mixed equals the ratio of the DISTANCE of the mean price from each price, but cross-matched — cheap quantity is proportional to (dear price − mean price), and dear quantity to (mean price − cheap price). cheap:dear = (₹60−₹48):(₹48−₹40) = 12:8 = 3:2. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q227"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 3, "q": "Rice at ₹15/kg is mixed with rice at ₹25/kg so that the mixture costs ₹20/kg. The mixing ratio (cheap:dear) is:", "opts": ["15:25","1:1*","1:1","2:1"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The rule of alligation says: the ratio in which two ingredients must be mixed equals the ratio of the DISTANCE of the mean price from each price, but cross-matched — cheap quantity is proportional to (dear price − mean price), and dear quantity to (mean price − cheap price). cheap:dear = (₹25−₹20):(₹20−₹15) = 5:5 = 1:1. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q228"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 3, "q": "Rice at ₹20/kg is mixed with rice at ₹30/kg so that the mixture costs ₹25/kg. The mixing ratio (cheap:dear) is:", "opts": ["1:1*","20:30","1:1","2:1"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The rule of alligation says: the ratio in which two ingredients must be mixed equals the ratio of the DISTANCE of the mean price from each price, but cross-matched — cheap quantity is proportional to (dear price − mean price), and dear quantity to (mean price − cheap price). cheap:dear = (₹30−₹25):(₹25−₹20) = 5:5 = 1:1. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q229"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "A student needs 35% to pass an exam of 500 marks. He scores 165 and fails by 10 marks. The pass mark is:", "opts": ["165","185","175","250"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The pass mark is simply the required percentage applied to the full marks — the \"scores X, fails by Y\" detail is just there to let you cross-check your answer. Pass mark = 35% of 500 = 500 × 35/100 = 175. Check: he scored 165, and 165 + 10 (the shortfall) = 175, which matches 175. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q230"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "averages", "diff": 2, "q": "The average weight of 20 students is 28 kg. When a teacher joins, the average increases by 3 kg. The teacher's weight is:", "opts": ["104 kg","88 kg","91 kg","31 kg"], "ans": 2, "exp": "When one new member joins and raises the average, that member must be heavy enough to cover their own share of the new average PLUS make up the \"rise\" for every existing member. New average = 28 + 3 = 31 kg. Teacher's weight = new average + (old count × rise) = 31 + 20×3 = 91 kg. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["averages"], "id": "Q231"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "If 40% of a number is 400, the number is:", "opts": ["160","500","1000","2000"], "ans": 2, "exp": "This reverses the usual percentage question: instead of finding a part, you're given the part and must recover the whole. Since part = whole × pct/100, dividing both sides by pct/100 gives whole = part × 100/pct. Number = 400 × 100/40 = 1000. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q232"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "profit", "diff": 1, "q": "After a discount of 20% on the marked price ₹1,200, the selling price is:", "opts": ["₹960","₹1,440","₹240","₹900"], "ans": 0, "exp": "A discount of r% means the buyer pays only (100−r)% of the marked price. SP = MP × (100−20)/100 = ₹1,200 × 80/100 = ₹960. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["profit"], "id": "Q233"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 3, "q": "A invests ₹15,000 for 12 months; B joins with ₹10,000 for the last 6 months. Their profit-sharing ratio is:", "opts": ["1:3","12:6","3:1","3:2"], "ans": 2, "exp": "When partners invest for different lengths of time, profit isn't split by capital alone — it's split by \"capital × time invested\", since a rupee invested for longer contributes more to the business. A's share ∝ ₹15,000×12 = 180000; B's share ∝ ₹10,000×6 = 60000; ratio = 180000:60000 = 3:1 in lowest terms. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q234"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "tsd", "diff": 1, "q": "90 km/h expressed in metres per second is:", "opts": ["25 m/s","27 m/s","45 m/s","324 m/s"], "ans": 0, "exp": "1 km/h = 1000 m / 3600 s = 5/18 m/s, so to convert km/h to m/s you multiply by 5/18 (and to go the other way, multiply by 18/5). 90 × 5/18 = 25 m/s. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["tsd"], "id": "Q235"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "tsd", "diff": 3, "q": "A man goes to office at 30 km/h and returns by the same route at 60 km/h. His average speed for the whole journey is:", "opts": ["45 km/h","36 km/h","40 km/h","44 km/h"], "ans": 2, "exp": "When the SAME distance is covered at two different speeds, average speed is NOT the simple arithmetic mean — it's the harmonic mean, because more time is spent travelling at the slower speed. Average speed = 2ab/(a+b) = 2×30×60/(30+60) = 40 km/h (this will always be less than the plain average of 30 and 60, which is 45). Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["tsd"], "id": "Q236"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 1, "q": "The price of an article rises from ₹250 to what value after a 10% increase?", "opts": ["₹25","₹275","₹287","₹262"], "ans": 1, "exp": "A percentage increase means adding that fraction of the original value back onto itself. Increase = ₹250 × 10/100 = ₹25; new price = ₹250 + ₹25 = ₹275. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q237"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "sqroots", "diff": 2, "q": "√50 simplified is:", "opts": ["√50","6√2","25√2","5√2"], "ans": 3, "exp": "To simplify a surd, pull out the largest perfect-square factor hiding inside the number, since √(a×b) = √a × √b. 50 = 25×2, and 25 is a perfect square (5²), so √50 = √25·√2 = 5√2. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["sqroots"], "id": "Q238"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "interest", "diff": 3, "q": "The difference between CI and SI on ₹10,000 for 2 years at 5% p.a. is:", "opts": ["₹500","₹25","₹50","₹12"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Over 2 years, compound interest earns a little extra beyond simple interest because year 2's interest is calculated on (principal + year-1 interest), not just the principal — that \"interest on interest\" is exactly P×(R/100)². Difference = P×(R/100)² = ₹10,000 × (5/100)² = 25.000000000000004. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["interest"], "id": "Q239"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "interest", "diff": 2, "q": "The compound interest on ₹12,000 at 20% p.a. for 2 years (compounded annually) is:", "opts": ["₹4,800","₹17,280","₹7,680","₹5,280"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Compound interest re-invests each year's interest, so the amount after n years is Principal × (1 + Rate/100)ⁿ, and the interest earned is that amount minus the original principal. Amount = ₹12,000 × (1+20/100)² = 17280; CI = 17280 − ₹12,000 = 5280. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["interest"], "id": "Q240"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "work", "diff": 2, "q": "12 men can build a wall in 20 days. How many days will 6 men take?", "opts": ["20 days","30 days","40 days","46 days"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The total \"work\" (measured in man-days) needed to build the wall stays fixed, so more workers finish faster and fewer workers take longer — this is an inverse relationship, captured by M₁D₁ = M₂D₂. Total work = 12×20 = 240 man-days. With 6 men: D = 240/6 = 40 days. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["work"], "id": "Q241"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹8,000 and ₹10,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹3,600, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹1,800","₹1,600","₹2,000","₹1,400"], "ans": 1, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹8,000:₹10,000 = 4:5. A's share = 4/(4+5) × ₹3,600 = 4/9 × ₹3,600 = ₹1,600. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q242"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "ratio", "diff": 1, "q": "In a group of 210 people, men and women are in the ratio 5:2. The number of women is:", "opts": ["60","63","105","150"], "ans": 0, "exp": "A ratio a:b splits a total into (a+b) equal \"parts\"; each group gets as many parts as its ratio number. Total parts = 5+2 = 7. Women's share = 2/7 × 210 = 60. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["ratio"], "id": "Q243"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "profit", "diff": 1, "q": "After a discount of 10% on the marked price ₹1,000, the selling price is:", "opts": ["₹900","₹100","₹1,100","₹850"], "ans": 0, "exp": "A discount of r% means the buyer pays only (100−r)% of the marked price. SP = MP × (100−10)/100 = ₹1,000 × 90/100 = ₹900. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["profit"], "id": "Q244"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "interest", "diff": 1, "q": "The simple interest on ₹8,000 at 5% p.a. for 5 years is:", "opts": ["₹2,400","₹2,000","₹4,000","₹1,600"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Simple interest grows by the same fixed amount every year, calculated only on the original principal: SI = Principal × Rate × Time / 100. SI = ₹8,000 × 5 × 5 / 100 = 2000. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["interest"], "id": "Q245"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 3, "q": "A invests ₹15,000 for 12 months; B joins with ₹24,000 for the last 8 months. Their profit-sharing ratio is:", "opts": ["16:15","12:8","5:8","15:16"], "ans": 3, "exp": "When partners invest for different lengths of time, profit isn't split by capital alone — it's split by \"capital × time invested\", since a rupee invested for longer contributes more to the business. A's share ∝ ₹15,000×12 = 180000; B's share ∝ ₹24,000×8 = 192000; ratio = 180000:192000 = 15:16 in lowest terms. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q246"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 1, "q": "The price of an article rises from ₹800 to what value after a 20% increase?", "opts": ["₹880","₹1,000","₹160","₹960"], "ans": 3, "exp": "A percentage increase means adding that fraction of the original value back onto itself. Increase = ₹800 × 20/100 = ₹160; new price = ₹800 + ₹160 = ₹960. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q247"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "A student needs 45% to pass an exam of 600 marks. He scores 250 and fails by 20 marks. The pass mark is:", "opts": ["290","250","300","270"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The pass mark is simply the required percentage applied to the full marks — the \"scores X, fails by Y\" detail is just there to let you cross-check your answer. Pass mark = 45% of 600 = 600 × 45/100 = 270. Check: he scored 250, and 250 + 20 (the shortfall) = 270, which matches 270. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q248"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 1, "q": "What is 20% of 240?", "opts": ["50","96","46","48"], "ans": 3, "exp": "\"Percent\" literally means \"per hundred\", so X% of a number = that number × X/100. 20% of 240 = 240 × 20/100 = 48. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q249"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "averages", "diff": 2, "q": "A batsman's average after 10 innings is 50. After scoring 120 in the next innings, his new average is:", "opts": ["85","56.4","57.4","52"], "ans": 1, "exp": "To update an average after a new data point, first recover the OLD total (average × old count), add the new value, then divide by the new count — never just average the average with the new score. Old total runs = 50×10 = 500. New total = 500 + 120 = 620. New average = 620/11 = 56.4. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["averages"], "id": "Q250"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "averages", "diff": 2, "q": "A batsman's average after 20 innings is 50. After scoring 90 in the next innings, his new average is:", "opts": ["52.9","52","51.9","70"], "ans": 2, "exp": "To update an average after a new data point, first recover the OLD total (average × old count), add the new value, then divide by the new count — never just average the average with the new score. Old total runs = 50×20 = 1000. New total = 1000 + 90 = 1090. New average = 1090/21 = 51.9. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["averages"], "id": "Q251"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "sqroots", "diff": 1, "q": "The square root of 625 is:", "opts": ["25","24","26","27"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The square root of a perfect square is the number which, when multiplied by itself, produces that value — for exam speed, memorise squares up to 30² or use prime factorisation. 25² = 25×25 = 625, so √625 = 25. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["sqroots"], "id": "Q252"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "ratio", "diff": 1, "q": "Divide ₹500 between A and B in the ratio 2:3. A's share is:", "opts": ["₹250","₹200","₹150","₹300"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Splitting a sum in ratio a:b means dividing it into (a+b) equal parts and giving A exactly \"a\" of those parts. A's share = 2/(2+3) × ₹500 = 2/5 × ₹500 = ₹200. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["ratio"], "id": "Q253"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹6,000 and ₹10,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹3,200, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹1,200","₹2,000","₹1,400","₹1,600"], "ans": 0, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹6,000:₹10,000 = 3:5. A's share = 3/(3+5) × ₹3,200 = 3/8 × ₹3,200 = ₹1,200. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q254"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "tsd", "diff": 2, "q": "A train 150 m long runs at 54 km/h. Time taken to cross a pole is:", "opts": ["10 s","20 s","5 s","13 s"], "ans": 0, "exp": "\"Crossing a pole\" means the train must cover a distance equal to its own length (the pole itself has no length), so time = length ÷ speed — but first convert speed from km/h to m/s using the factor 5/18. Speed = 54×5/18 = 15 m/s; time = 150/15 = 10 s. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["tsd"], "id": "Q255"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 3, "q": "A invests ₹12,000 for 12 months; B joins with ₹18,000 for the last 8 months. Their profit-sharing ratio is:", "opts": ["12:8","1:1","2:1","2:3"], "ans": 1, "exp": "When partners invest for different lengths of time, profit isn't split by capital alone — it's split by \"capital × time invested\", since a rupee invested for longer contributes more to the business. A's share ∝ ₹12,000×12 = 144000; B's share ∝ ₹18,000×8 = 144000; ratio = 144000:144000 = 1:1 in lowest terms. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q256"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹6,000 and ₹10,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹8,000, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹5,000","₹3,000","₹3,200","₹4,000"], "ans": 1, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹6,000:₹10,000 = 3:5. A's share = 3/(3+5) × ₹8,000 = 3/8 × ₹8,000 = ₹3,000. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q257"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "A student needs 33% to pass an exam of 800 marks. He scores 254 and fails by 10 marks. The pass mark is:", "opts": ["274","400","254","264"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The pass mark is simply the required percentage applied to the full marks — the \"scores X, fails by Y\" detail is just there to let you cross-check your answer. Pass mark = 33% of 800 = 800 × 33/100 = 264. Check: he scored 254, and 254 + 10 (the shortfall) = 264, which matches 264. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q258"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "A student needs 35% to pass an exam of 600 marks. He scores 185 and fails by 25 marks. The pass mark is:", "opts": ["235","300","185","210"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The pass mark is simply the required percentage applied to the full marks — the \"scores X, fails by Y\" detail is just there to let you cross-check your answer. Pass mark = 35% of 600 = 600 × 35/100 = 210. Check: he scored 185, and 185 + 25 (the shortfall) = 210, which matches 210. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q259"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "A number is increased by 25% and then decreased by 10%. The net change is:", "opts": ["12.5% increase","18% increase","15% decrease","35% increase"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Successive percentage changes don't simply add up, because the second change acts on the ALREADY-changed value, not the original — this is the same idea as compound growth. Here the first change is +25% and the second is -10%, so net % change = a + b + (a×b)/100 = 25 + (-10) + (25×-10)/100 = 12.5%, i.e. a 12.5% increase. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q260"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "profit", "diff": 1, "q": "An article costing ₹500 is sold at a profit of 25%. The selling price is:", "opts": ["₹625","₹375","₹650","₹525"], "ans": 0, "exp": "A profit of p% on cost price means the selling price is (100+p)% of the cost price. SP = CP × (100+25)/100 = ₹500 × 125/100 = ₹625. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["profit"], "id": "Q261"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "If 40% of a number is 600, the number is:", "opts": ["240","1500","750","3000"], "ans": 1, "exp": "This reverses the usual percentage question: instead of finding a part, you're given the part and must recover the whole. Since part = whole × pct/100, dividing both sides by pct/100 gives whole = part × 100/pct. Number = 600 × 100/40 = 1500. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q262"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "interest", "diff": 2, "q": "The compound interest on ₹12,000 at 5% p.a. for 2 years (compounded annually) is:", "opts": ["₹1,830","₹1,200","₹13,230","₹1,230"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Compound interest re-invests each year's interest, so the amount after n years is Principal × (1 + Rate/100)ⁿ, and the interest earned is that amount minus the original principal. Amount = ₹12,000 × (1+5/100)² = 13230; CI = 13230 − ₹12,000 = 1230. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["interest"], "id": "Q263"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "tsd", "diff": 2, "q": "A train 150 m long runs at 72 km/h. Time taken to cross a pole is:", "opts": ["7.5 s","15 s","3.75 s","10.5 s"], "ans": 0, "exp": "\"Crossing a pole\" means the train must cover a distance equal to its own length (the pole itself has no length), so time = length ÷ speed — but first convert speed from km/h to m/s using the factor 5/18. Speed = 72×5/18 = 20 m/s; time = 150/20 = 7.5 s. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["tsd"], "id": "Q264"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 2, "q": "A mixture of 50 L has milk and water in the ratio 4:1. If 10 L of water is added, the new ratio is:", "opts": ["4:1","2:0","1:2","2:1"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Adding pure water changes only the water quantity — the milk quantity is untouched, so find the milk amount first (it stays fixed), then just add the extra water to the old water amount before re-forming the ratio. Milk = 4/5 × 50 = 40 L (unchanged). Water = 1/5 × 50 = 10 L, plus 10 L added = 20 L. New ratio = 40:20 = 2:1. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q265"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 1, "q": "What is 60% of 300?", "opts": ["180","183","360","177"], "ans": 0, "exp": "\"Percent\" literally means \"per hundred\", so X% of a number = that number × X/100. 60% of 300 = 300 × 60/100 = 180. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q266"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "work", "diff": 2, "q": "A finishes a work in 10 days and B in 15 days. Working together, they finish it in:", "opts": ["12 days","6 days","25 days","8 days"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Convert each person's time into a work-rate (fraction of the job done per day), add the rates together for teamwork, then invert the combined rate to get the combined time. Combined rate = 1/10 + 1/15 = 1/5.999999999999999; so together they take 5.999999999999999 days. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["work"], "id": "Q267"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹2,000 and ₹3,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹5,000, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹2,000","₹3,000","₹2,500","₹2,100"], "ans": 0, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹2,000:₹3,000 = 2:3. A's share = 2/(2+3) × ₹5,000 = 2/5 × ₹5,000 = ₹2,000. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q268"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "sqroots", "diff": 1, "q": "The square root of 225 is:", "opts": ["16","17","14","15"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The square root of a perfect square is the number which, when multiplied by itself, produces that value — for exam speed, memorise squares up to 30² or use prime factorisation. 15² = 15×15 = 225, so √225 = 15. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["sqroots"], "id": "Q269"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "profit", "diff": 1, "q": "An article costing ₹500 is sold at a profit of 20%. The selling price is:", "opts": ["₹625","₹520","₹400","₹600"], "ans": 3, "exp": "A profit of p% on cost price means the selling price is (100+p)% of the cost price. SP = CP × (100+20)/100 = ₹500 × 120/100 = ₹600. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["profit"], "id": "Q270"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "averages", "diff": 2, "q": "The average of the consecutive odd numbers 31, 33, 35, 37, 39, 41, 43 is:", "opts": ["43","37","39","31"], "ans": 1, "exp": "For any set of evenly/equally spaced numbers (like consecutive odd numbers), the terms are symmetric around the middle value, so the average always equals the middle term — no need to add everything up. Here the 7 numbers are 31, 33, 35, 37, 39, 41, 43; the middle term is 37. Check: sum = 259, and 259/7 = 37.0, matching the middle term. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["averages"], "id": "Q271"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 1, "q": "What is 12% of 360?", "opts": ["46","86","43","40"], "ans": 2, "exp": "\"Percent\" literally means \"per hundred\", so X% of a number = that number × X/100. 12% of 360 = 360 × 12/100 = 43.2. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q272"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹10,000 and ₹15,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹4,000, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹2,000","₹1,600","₹2,100","₹2,400"], "ans": 1, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹10,000:₹15,000 = 2:3. A's share = 2/(2+3) × ₹4,000 = 2/5 × ₹4,000 = ₹1,600. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q273"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹15,000 and ₹20,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹4,200, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹2,400","₹2,300","₹2,100","₹1,800"], "ans": 3, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹15,000:₹20,000 = 3:4. A's share = 3/(3+4) × ₹4,200 = 3/7 × ₹4,200 = ₹1,800. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q274"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 2, "q": "A mixture of 100 L has milk and water in the ratio 4:1. If 40 L of water is added, the new ratio is:", "opts": ["4:1","4:3","5:2","3:4"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Adding pure water changes only the water quantity — the milk quantity is untouched, so find the milk amount first (it stays fixed), then just add the extra water to the old water amount before re-forming the ratio. Milk = 4/5 × 100 = 80 L (unchanged). Water = 1/5 × 100 = 20 L, plus 40 L added = 60 L. New ratio = 80:60 = 4:3. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q275"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹3,000 and ₹5,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹4,800, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹1,800","₹2,400","₹1,900","₹3,000"], "ans": 0, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹3,000:₹5,000 = 3:5. A's share = 3/(3+5) × ₹4,800 = 3/8 × ₹4,800 = ₹1,800. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q276"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 3, "q": "A invests ₹20,000 for 12 months; B joins with ₹18,000 for the last 8 months. Their profit-sharing ratio is:", "opts": ["5:3","12:8","10:9","3:5"], "ans": 0, "exp": "When partners invest for different lengths of time, profit isn't split by capital alone — it's split by \"capital × time invested\", since a rupee invested for longer contributes more to the business. A's share ∝ ₹20,000×12 = 240000; B's share ∝ ₹18,000×8 = 144000; ratio = 240000:144000 = 5:3 in lowest terms. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q277"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "If 30% of a number is 400, the number is:", "opts": ["2666","1333","120","666"], "ans": 1, "exp": "This reverses the usual percentage question: instead of finding a part, you're given the part and must recover the whole. Since part = whole × pct/100, dividing both sides by pct/100 gives whole = part × 100/pct. Number = 400 × 100/30 = 1333.3333333333333. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q278"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "averages", "diff": 2, "q": "The average weight of 25 students is 22 kg. When a teacher joins, the average increases by 3 kg. The teacher's weight is:", "opts": ["97 kg","25 kg","100 kg","91 kg"], "ans": 2, "exp": "When one new member joins and raises the average, that member must be heavy enough to cover their own share of the new average PLUS make up the \"rise\" for every existing member. New average = 22 + 3 = 25 kg. Teacher's weight = new average + (old count × rise) = 25 + 25×3 = 100 kg. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["averages"], "id": "Q279"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "averages", "diff": 1, "q": "The average of 5 numbers is 20. Their sum is:", "opts": ["105","80","100","20"], "ans": 2, "exp": "Average is defined as sum ÷ count, so rearranging gives sum = average × count — the reverse of the usual averaging step. Sum = count × average = 5 × 20 = 100. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["averages"], "id": "Q280"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "interest", "diff": 2, "q": "At 4% p.a. simple interest, a sum earns ₹600 in 5 years. The principal is:", "opts": ["₹3,600","₹6,000","₹3,000","₹1,500"], "ans": 2, "exp": "This is the SI formula solved backwards: since SI = P×R×T/100, the principal is P = SI×100/(R×T). P = 600×100/(4×5) = 3000. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["interest"], "id": "Q281"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "A number is increased by 10% and then decreased by 50%. The net change is:", "opts": ["60% increase","50% decrease","40% decrease","45% decrease"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Successive percentage changes don't simply add up, because the second change acts on the ALREADY-changed value, not the original — this is the same idea as compound growth. Here the first change is +10% and the second is -50%, so net % change = a + b + (a×b)/100 = 10 + (-50) + (10×-50)/100 = -45.0%, i.e. a 45.0% decrease. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q282"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 3, "q": "Rice at ₹24/kg is mixed with rice at ₹36/kg so that the mixture costs ₹30/kg. The mixing ratio (cheap:dear) is:", "opts": ["2:1","1:1","1:1*","24:36"], "ans": 1, "exp": "The rule of alligation says: the ratio in which two ingredients must be mixed equals the ratio of the DISTANCE of the mean price from each price, but cross-matched — cheap quantity is proportional to (dear price − mean price), and dear quantity to (mean price − cheap price). cheap:dear = (₹36−₹30):(₹30−₹24) = 6:6 = 1:1. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q283"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "numbers", "diff": 1, "q": "The HCF of 16 and 40 is:", "opts": ["80","8","4","16"], "ans": 1, "exp": "HCF (Highest Common Factor) is the largest number that divides both given numbers exactly with no remainder — list common factors, or repeatedly divide (Euclid's method), and take the biggest one. Factors common to 16 and 40 top out at 8, so HCF(16, 40) = 8. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["numbers"], "id": "Q284"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "profit", "diff": 1, "q": "After a discount of 10% on the marked price ₹600, the selling price is:", "opts": ["₹510","₹660","₹60","₹540"], "ans": 3, "exp": "A discount of r% means the buyer pays only (100−r)% of the marked price. SP = MP × (100−10)/100 = ₹600 × 90/100 = ₹540. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["profit"], "id": "Q285"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹4,000 and ₹5,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹9,000, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹5,000","₹4,500","₹4,000","₹4,100"], "ans": 2, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹4,000:₹5,000 = 4:5. A's share = 4/(4+5) × ₹9,000 = 4/9 × ₹9,000 = ₹4,000. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q286"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 3, "q": "A invests ₹15,000 for 12 months; B joins with ₹10,000 for the last 9 months. Their profit-sharing ratio is:", "opts": ["12:9","2:1","1:2","3:2"], "ans": 1, "exp": "When partners invest for different lengths of time, profit isn't split by capital alone — it's split by \"capital × time invested\", since a rupee invested for longer contributes more to the business. A's share ∝ ₹15,000×12 = 180000; B's share ∝ ₹10,000×9 = 90000; ratio = 180000:90000 = 2:1 in lowest terms. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q287"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹20,000 and ₹25,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹9,000, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹5,000","₹4,000","₹4,500","₹3,500"], "ans": 1, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹20,000:₹25,000 = 4:5. A's share = 4/(4+5) × ₹9,000 = 4/9 × ₹9,000 = ₹4,000. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q288"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "sqroots", "diff": 2, "q": "√1.44 = ?", "opts": ["1.2","0.12","1.3","12"], "ans": 0, "exp": "To find the square root of a decimal, notice it's the number which, multiplied by itself, gives back the original value — for a clean decimal like this, guess-and-check with one decimal place usually lands exactly. 1.2 × 1.2 = 1.44, so √1.44 = 1.2. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers. Tip: count decimal places — a perfect square with 2 decimal digits usually has a root with 1 decimal digit.", "tags": ["sqroots"], "id": "Q289"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "averages", "diff": 2, "q": "A batsman's average after 12 innings is 56. After scoring 120 in the next innings, his new average is:", "opts": ["58","61.9","60.9","88"], "ans": 2, "exp": "To update an average after a new data point, first recover the OLD total (average × old count), add the new value, then divide by the new count — never just average the average with the new score. Old total runs = 56×12 = 672. New total = 672 + 120 = 792. New average = 792/13 = 60.9. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["averages"], "id": "Q290"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "profit", "diff": 1, "q": "An article costing ₹500 is sold at a profit of 10%. The selling price is:", "opts": ["₹550","₹450","₹575","₹510"], "ans": 0, "exp": "A profit of p% on cost price means the selling price is (100+p)% of the cost price. SP = CP × (100+10)/100 = ₹500 × 110/100 = ₹550. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["profit"], "id": "Q291"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "mixture", "diff": 2, "q": "A mixture of 40 L has milk and water in the ratio 3:1. If 10 L of water is added, the new ratio is:", "opts": ["3:2","3:1","4:2","2:3"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Adding pure water changes only the water quantity — the milk quantity is untouched, so find the milk amount first (it stays fixed), then just add the extra water to the old water amount before re-forming the ratio. Milk = 3/4 × 40 = 30 L (unchanged). Water = 1/4 × 40 = 10 L, plus 10 L added = 20 L. New ratio = 30:20 = 3:2. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["mixture"], "id": "Q292"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹6,000 and ₹10,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹6,400, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹3,200","₹2,400","₹4,000","₹2,600"], "ans": 1, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹6,000:₹10,000 = 3:5. A's share = 3/(3+5) × ₹6,400 = 3/8 × ₹6,400 = ₹2,400. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q293"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "averages", "diff": 2, "q": "A batsman's average after 20 innings is 58. After scoring 110 in the next innings, his new average is:", "opts": ["84","60","60.5","61.5"], "ans": 2, "exp": "To update an average after a new data point, first recover the OLD total (average × old count), add the new value, then divide by the new count — never just average the average with the new score. Old total runs = 58×20 = 1160. New total = 1160 + 110 = 1270. New average = 1270/21 = 60.5. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["averages"], "id": "Q294"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "ratio", "diff": 2, "q": "The present ages of two brothers are in the ratio 5:6. If the younger is 20 years old, the elder's age is:", "opts": ["42","24","34","22"], "ans": 1, "exp": "A ratio like a:b means the two ages are \"a units\" and \"b units\" of some common unit — find the unit from the age you already know, then scale up the other side. One ratio unit = 20/5 = 4 years, so elder's age = 6 × 4 = 24 years. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["ratio"], "id": "Q295"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "tsd", "diff": 2, "q": "A train 120 m long runs at 72 km/h. Time taken to cross a pole is:", "opts": ["6 s","3 s","12 s","9 s"], "ans": 0, "exp": "\"Crossing a pole\" means the train must cover a distance equal to its own length (the pole itself has no length), so time = length ÷ speed — but first convert speed from km/h to m/s using the factor 5/18. Speed = 72×5/18 = 20 m/s; time = 120/20 = 6 s. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["tsd"], "id": "Q296"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "ratio", "diff": 2, "q": "The present ages of two brothers are in the ratio 3:4. If the younger is 15 years old, the elder's age is:", "opts": ["20","12","26","17"], "ans": 0, "exp": "A ratio like a:b means the two ages are \"a units\" and \"b units\" of some common unit — find the unit from the age you already know, then scale up the other side. One ratio unit = 15/3 = 5 years, so elder's age = 4 × 5 = 20 years. Common mistake: mixing up which number to divide by which — write the formula first, then plug in numbers.", "tags": ["ratio"], "id": "Q297"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "ratio", "diff": 2, "q": "The present ages of two brothers are in the ratio 5:6. If the younger is 25 years old, the elder's age is:", "opts": ["38","27","30","18"], "ans": 2, "exp": "A ratio like a:b means the two ages are \"a units\" and \"b units\" of some common unit — find the unit from the age you already know, then scale up the other side. One ratio unit = 25/5 = 5 years, so elder's age = 6 × 5 = 30 years. Faster mental math: round the numbers first to estimate the answer, then compute exactly to confirm.", "tags": ["ratio"], "id": "Q298"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "percentage", "diff": 2, "q": "A number is increased by 10% and then decreased by 10%. The net change is:", "opts": ["20% increase","1% decrease","0% decrease","6% decrease"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Successive percentage changes don't simply add up, because the second change acts on the ALREADY-changed value, not the original — this is the same idea as compound growth. Here the first change is +10% and the second is -10%, so net % change = a + b + (a×b)/100 = 10 + (-10) + (10×-10)/100 = -1.0%, i.e. a 1.0% decrease. Watch the units and rounding — SSB papers often hide the trap in a rounding step.", "tags": ["percentage"], "id": "Q299"},
{"sec": "C", "topic": "partnership", "diff": 2, "q": "A and B invest ₹3,000 and ₹4,000 in a business. Out of a profit of ₹2,800, A's share is:", "opts": ["₹1,200","₹1,400","₹1,300","₹1,600"], "ans": 0, "exp": "When both partners invest for the same length of time, profit is shared strictly in proportion to how much capital each put in. Capital ratio = ₹3,000:₹4,000 = 3:4. A's share = 3/(3+4) × ₹2,800 = 3/7 × ₹2,800 = ₹1,200. Exam tip: always double-check which quantity is the 'whole' and which is the 'part' before dividing.", "tags": ["partnership"], "id": "Q300"}
